Give each sensor timer its own callback group, not one shared group.

0001 fixed a data race by making the single shared callback group MutuallyExclusive. That
is correct but too coarse: ONE MutuallyExclusive group serialises EVERY callback in it, so
the state timer queues behind image fetches.

Measured (drone-sim, 2026-08-02) with 640x480 Scene+Depth at 20 Hz:

    requested state timer  333 Hz  (update_airsim_control_every_n_sec = 0.003)
    achieved               ~35 Hz
    RPC cost of one state cycle  0.26 ms  ->  a 3854 Hz ceiling

So the limit was not the RPC and not the simulator -- it was queueing behind a ~50 ms image
callback in the same group. IMU, GPS, magnetometer and odometry all ride the state timer, so
all of them were capped at the image rate.

The race 0001 fixed was drone_state_timer_cb RE-ENTERING ITSELF (30% of consecutive log
timestamps were out of order for a single vehicle). A per-timer MutuallyExclusive group still
prevents that -- a callback cannot re-enter its own group -- while allowing different timers to
run concurrently on the MultiThreadedExecutor. That is the property actually needed.

The groups are stored as members because rclcpp's node holds only weak references to callback
groups; a locally-scoped shared_ptr would be freed and the timer silently orphaned.

This is an upstream defect and is worth reporting to Cosys-Lab together with 0001 and 0002.

NOTE ON LINE ENDINGS: Cosys-AirSim sources are CRLF. This hunk was GENERATED from the real
files, not hand-written -- a hand-written LF hunk fails to apply.

